(project pricklypear-jobs
 (doc "Pricklypear book chapter: jobs — durable background jobs, step checkpoints, and cron; host owns concurrency, guests see rows")
 (status implemented)
 (* agent note (|2026-12 plan pass: flipped planned -> implemented. All four deliverable subsections (node-shapes, runner, step-checkpoints, schedules) are implemented and live: image/lib/jobs.ml (5-field cron + every-ms, SKIP LOCKED claim, runner fiber forked in boot.ml). The prim surface lives in image/lib/prims_jobs.ml (split out of prims_db.ml; prims_db.ml just calls Prims_jobs.register): submit-job/submit-job-at/job-status/cancel-job/jobs/schedule/unschedule/schedules. The label lagged the ship.|) *)
 (* agent note (|2026-10 lease rework: 015's recovery rule was "status=running AND runner_id <> mine ⇒ dead runner", which is only sound for a single claimant — boot.ml reaped on EVERY loop iteration, so a second worker (or a second image on the same DB) requeued the other's live rows on each 1s tick. 045_jobs_lease.sql adds lease_heartbeat_at/lease_deadline and the reclaim predicate becomes "lease_deadline has PASSED", never "the owner is someone else": a live lease is never stolen. runner_id is now `boot-<host>#<pid>` (host-scoped) purely for FAST same-host restart recovery (preempt_dead_host_leases: my host + dead pid ⇒ recover now instead of waiting out the TTL); a foreign host's lease is untouched. Terminal writes (record_done/record_failed) are CASes on (status=running AND runner_id=owner), so a worker whose lease lapsed cannot overwrite a newer attempt. Expired leases requeue IMMEDIATELY (run_at=now, no backoff) — attempts was already incremented at claim, so the retry budget bounds a job that keeps killing its runner. SUPERSEDES: the quoted "run_at = now + backoff(attempts)" in the runner loop's RECORD step still applies to EVAL FAILURES; lease-expiry recovery is the no-backoff path.|) *)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section jobs

  (doc "Durable jobs as typed data nodes; a host-owned runner fiber; step checkpoints for exactly-once; schedules for cron. The async story for Nopales is job-shaped, not call-shaped.")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        =========================================================
        WHY THIS CHAPTER EXISTS
        =========================================================
        A web framework needs background tasks and cron. AI calls
        take minutes; renders take longer; backups and feed polls
        must happen with no request in flight. Today every such
        feature would hand-roll its own deferral, and the book
        already carries three fragments of the same idea:
        film-edit's job-first vocabulary (gen-submit / gen-status),
        image.primitives-layer's deferred "host cron/scheduler
        table", and one-tree's user-keyed data placement. This
        chapter is THE async substrate: one design that those
        surfaces build on.

        The shape is dictated by decisions already made:
          - ONE IMAGE PROCESS — the worker is an in-process host
            fiber, not a fleet. No broker, no Redis, no worker
            tier (the Celery shape is a symptom of stateless
            web workers; we have the opposite constraint).
          - THE TREE IS TRUTH — jobs are typed data nodes, not
            rows in a queue table. Inspection, backlinks,
            provenance, and /data browsing come free.
            (SUPERSEDED — see the node-shapes reconcile note: v1
            ships a DEDICATED jobs table, migrations/015_jobs.sql,
            not tree nodes. Kept here as the original plan's
            rationale; the dedicated-table design below records why
            the implementation diverged.)
          - HOST OWNS CONCURRENCY — Nopales has no fiber/async
            API and gains none. Guests submit jobs and read job
            rows; the host runner fiber calls back into eval
            through the same Control rim an HTTP request uses.

        =========================================================
        THE MODEL: CALLERS AND CALLEES
        =========================================================
        Eval is always direct-style and request-shaped. What
        varies is WHO CALLS:

          HTTP request  ─┐
          REPL line      ─┼─▶  Control.perform_prim  ─▶  rim (Eio fiber)
          runner fiber   ─┘     (one-shot effect)         does the I/O

        The runner fiber is a third caller into the same
        dispatch. From a job function's perspective a job run
        is indistinguishable from someone hitting a route:
        same prims, same budget accounting, same with-transaction
        semantics. No guest-visible suspension exists at any
        point in this design.

        =========================================================
        NODE SHAPES (one-tree placement — RECONCILED 2026-08)
        =========================================================
        RECONCILE: the implementation ships a DEDICATED table, not
        tree nodes. migrations/015_jobs.sql + image/lib/jobs.ml +
        the boot-time runner fiber (image/lib/boot.ml) deliberately
        chose a dedicated `jobs` table (plus `job_steps` and
        `schedules`) over tree_nodes/data.shared.job.* for three
        reasons, per the 015_jobs.sql header comment:
          1. SKIP LOCKED CLAIM PROTOCOL — the runner's due-scan
             ('UPDATE ... WHERE status='pending' AND run_at<=now
             ORDER BY run_at FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED LIMIT 1') needs
             real typed status/run_at columns and a partial claim
             index (jobs_claim_idx ON jobs(run_at,id) WHERE
             status='pending'). A matpath subtree scan over
             data.shared.job.* cannot drive that partial index and
             would force a tree walk per claim's ORDER-BY.
          2. KERNEL AUTHORITY — migrations are the git-source-of-
             truth kernel tier (AGENTS.md branching authority); job
             rows are kernel state, so they belong to a migration
             table, not a branch-lens Nopales type.
          3. STRONGER BRANCHING EDGE — with a dedicated table (and
             no branch id on the rows), fork/merge/promote
             STRUCTURALLY cannot copy, diff, or move a job — the
             'NEVER participates in branch lenses' hard edge holds
             by construction, as originally intended.
        The tree-truth aspiration (inspection/backlinks//data
        browsing) is NOT met for jobs in v1: jobs are inspected via
        the job prims + /data is not a job surface. This is an
        accepted v1 tradeoff (kernel-backed queue state); a future
        projection onto tree nodes is possible but not planned.

        The original tree placement (kept for the decision trail):

          job        data.shared.job.<id>
          job-step   data.shared.job-step.<job-id>.<step-name>
          schedule   data.shared.job-schedule.<name>

        Shared placement was deliberate: the runner (host identity)
        must claim and mutate job rows regardless of who submitted,
        and path-prefix confinement on data.u.<user>.* would make
        the runner a privilege exception. Visibility is enforced at
        the PRIM layer instead: (jobs) and (job-status id) confine
        to the caller's submitted_by unless the caller is admin —
        same rule the notes garden applies to its shared indexes.
        submitted_by / branch / run_at are set at submit time and
        are NOT updatable through the CRUD prims (the runner owns
        status transitions).

        job fields (migrations/015_jobs.sql + 045_jobs_lease.sql):
          id           uuid     PK, gen_random_uuid at submit
          fn           string   name of a 1-arity Nopales fn
          args         json     single argument value
          branch       string   code lens captured at submit time
          submitted_by string   user who submitted (visibility key)
          status       enum     pending | running | done | failed | dead
          run_at       epoch-ms earliest start
          attempts     int      runs so far
          max_attempts int      default 3; exhausted ⇒ dead
          result       json     return value on done
          error        string   last failure message
          runner_id    string   lease OWNER: `boot-<host>#<pid>` of the
                                claiming process (host-scoped so a booting
                                image can recognise its own dead pids)
          lease_heartbeat_at epoch-ms last renewal by the owner
          lease_deadline     epoch-ms expiry; 0 = no live claim. A row is
                             recoverable only once this has PASSED (045)
          created_at   timestamptz submit time (list ordering)

        job-step fields (checkpoint rows):
          job_id, step_name, value json, finished_at epoch-ms

        schedule fields:
          name, fn, args, branch, cron string (5-field) OR every-ms
          int, next_run epoch-ms, enabled bool

        =========================================================
        GUEST PRIMS (the whole Nopales surface)
        =========================================================
          (submit-job "fn" args)           → job-id; captures caller's
                                             branch + identity; run_at = now
          (submit-job-at "fn" args when)   → job-id; when = epoch-ms
          (job-status id)                  → the job row (confined)
          (cancel-job id)                  → pending ⇒ failed/cancelled
          (jobs)                           → caller's jobs, recent first
          (with-step "name" (lambda () body))  → checkpoint boundary;
                                             inside a job: memoized by
                                             (job-id, name); outside a
                                             job: plain function call
          (NOTE: named with-step, not step — libs/rb already defines a
          `step` recipe-DSL function; reserving the flat name `step` for a
          kernel special form would clobber that library)
          (schedule "name" "cron-or-ms" "fn" args)   → upsert
          (unschedule "name")              → disable
          (schedules)                      → list

        Capability effects: submit/cancel/with-step are WriteDb;
        status/jobs/schedules are ReadDb; schedule/unschedule are
        WriteDb. Job FUNCTIONS carry whatever effects their bodies
        carry — inference + badges work unchanged because a job
        body is ordinary Nopales on an ordinary branch.

        THERE IS NO await-job. Guests never block on a job; they
        poll rows (htmx fragment refresh in the browser, job-status
        in the REPL). Result delivery is pull, not push — the same
        vocabulary film-edit specified for gen/render.

        =========================================================
        THE RUNNER (host side)
        =========================================================
        One Eio fiber forked at boot, beside the HTTP accept loop
        and REPL. Loop:

          1. CLAIM — due jobs (status=pending, run_at <= now),
             oldest first, claimed with SELECT ... FOR UPDATE
             SKIP LOCKED + status→running + runner_id, committed
             before eval begins. The claim is a LEASE: the same
             statement stamps runner_id (owner), lease_heartbeat_at
             and lease_deadline = now + ttl. SKIP LOCKED is correct
             from day one and is exactly the claim protocol an
             out-of-process worker (the Blender bridge shape, or a
             lens client) runs against the same rows today.
          2. EVAL — resolve fn on the job's recorded branch, call
             it with args, under a synthetic request context
             (whoami = submitted_by, branch = recorded branch) —
             the same session record the REPL fabricates, no HTTP
             request involved. Jobs run unbudgeted (max_int); see
             the budget note below.
          3. HEARTBEAT — a fork_daemon renews the lease every
             ttl/3 while eval runs (PP_JOB_HEARTBEAT_SECS, default
             20s). A renewal that matches no row means the lease
             was LOST; the runner logs it and the eval's terminal
             write is refused (see RECORD).
          4. RECORD — success ⇒ status=done + result; exception ⇒
             attempts+1 and either status=pending with
             run_at = now + backoff(attempts), or status=dead when
             attempts = max_attempts. Both terminal writes are
             CASes on (status='running' AND runner_id=owner): a
             worker whose lease lapsed can never overwrite a newer
             attempt's status or result.

        WAKE-UP: sleep until min(run_at) of pending jobs, with a
        bounded poll interval (tunable, default seconds) as the
        floor so earlier inserts are never missed by a stale
        sleep target. v1 has no LISTEN/NOTIFY.

        RECOVERY (045 — lease, not identity): the reclaim rule is
        "the lease deadline passed", NOT "the owner is someone
        else". A live lease is never stolen, whoever holds it, so
        N workers of any kind can claim from the same queue
        without reaping each other's live rows — the reason 015's
        runner_id comparison ("running AND runner_id <> mine ⇒
        dead") could not be kept: it is only correct for a single
        claimant, and boot.ml reaped on every 1s tick.

        An expired lease is requeued IMMEDIATELY (run_at = now, no
        backoff) or marked dead at max_attempts. attempts was
        already incremented by the claim, so the retry budget — not
        a timer — bounds a job that keeps killing its runner.

        Same-host restart stays FAST: runner_id is
        `boot-<host>#<pid>`, so at boot the runner recovers leases
        whose owner is THIS machine and whose pid is gone
        (preempt_dead_host_leases) in milliseconds instead of
        waiting out the TTL. A foreign host's lease is untouched
        even then. Residual risk is pid reuse across a restart: it
        costs latency (the row waits for the deadline), never
        correctness. Rows written before 045 have
        lease_deadline = 0 (already expired), so an upgrade
        recovers them on the first reap with no backfill.

        THE FLEET QUESTION. "One image, one runner fiber" is still
        the shipped topology, but it is now an OPERATIONAL choice,
        not something the schema enforces. Any process that speaks
        claim/heartbeat/CAS against these rows is a valid worker:
        the claim is SKIP LOCKED, liveness is a lease, and a late
        writer loses the CAS. What a second worker does NOT get is
        a fair share — single FIFO queue, no priorities or rate
        limits (hard edges below) — and a job that wedges a worker
        still wedges that worker.

        =========================================================
        STEP CHECKPOINTS (the DBOS idea, Nopales-shaped)
        =========================================================
        A job that dies at minute 9 of 10 should not re-spend
        minutes 1–8. (with-step "name" thunk) is the boundary:

          - First execution of (job-id, "name"): run the thunk,
            write the checkpoint row, return the value.
          - Re-execution (retry after crash): the checkpoint row
            exists ⇒ return its value WITHOUT running the thunk.

        Steps compose with with-transaction to give EXACTLY-ONCE
        for DB-writing steps, the DBOS co-location trick in our
        idiom: open one dynamic transaction, run the thunk (all
        its creates/updates ride the same connection), write the
        checkpoint row in the SAME transaction, commit. Either the
        writes and the checkpoint both land or neither does — the
        step provably never double-executes. The guest never sees
        a transaction handle; exactly-once is the default shape of
        (with-transaction (lambda () (with-step …))), not a discipline.

        Resume is by CHECKPOINT LOOKUP, not code replay: there is
        no determinism constraint on the job body. The one rule:
        a step name is a stable string within one job definition —
        (with-step "name" …) enforces this syntactically. Job code is
        pinned at submit time (branch + fn name), so a redefine on
        main mid-job does not change what a running retry resolves.

        v1 steps are RETRY-scoped: they shorten re-execution of a
        retried job; they do not suspend a live eval and resume it
        across boots mid-run. A crash mid-job restarts the job
        function from its top, replaying checkpoints.

        =========================================================
        SCHEDULES (cron)
        =========================================================
        Cron is sugar over the same machinery: a schedule row with
        next_run; the runner's due-scan treats due schedules like
        due jobs — submit the job, then compute the next next_run
        from the cron string or every-ms interval and write it back.
        One queue, one claim path, one recovery story. Missed fires
        while the image was down collapse to ONE catch-up run at
        boot (a backup that was down for three days runs once, not
        seventy times).

        =========================================================
        HARD EDGES (non-goals for v1)
        =========================================================
        - NO guest-visible fibers, promises, or await. Host owns
          concurrency; guests see rows.
        - NO in-image out-of-process isolation. A job that wedges
          in a blocking rim call can stall the image; eval budgets
          bound runaway Nopales, dead-runner recovery bounds
          crashes, and genuinely long external work belongs to an
          external worker speaking the same SKIP LOCKED claim
          (film-edit's Blender bridge is the precedent shape).
        - NO multiple queues, priorities, rate limits, unique-job
          constraints. Single FIFO queue, at-least-once at the job
          level, exactly-once at the transactional-step level.
        - NO mid-job guest notification. htmx polling or
          job-status reads only.
        - Job state NEVER participates in branch lenses. Paths
          contain no branch id; fork/merge/promote structurally
          cannot copy, diff, or move a job (one-tree branching-
          interaction stands by construction).

        =========================================================
        RELATIONSHIP TO EXISTING CHAPTERS
        =========================================================
        - film-edit: gen-submit / gen-status / render-job become
          applications of THIS substrate (a render job = a job row
          whose fn calls the Blender bridge); film-edit keeps its
          memoization-by-hash cache, which is orthogonal.
        - image.primitives-layer: the deferred "host cron/
          scheduler table" is this chapter's schedule nodes.
        - one-tree: jobs are the first shared-NAMESPACE data type
          placed under data.shared.* by design; the placement and
          its prim-level confinement are specified above.
        - lisp-interpreter.effects: new prims join the prim →
          effect-set table; nothing else about inference changes.
   |))
  )

  (subsection node-shapes
   (doc "job / job-step / schedule rows in a dedicated migration table (015_jobs.sql): field contracts, runner-owned status transitions, SKIP LOCKED claim index; v1 is a dedicated table, not data.shared tree nodes (see details RECONCILE note)")
   (status implemented)
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (test "image/test: jobs_test.ml node-shapes — job node create/claim/status transitions (submit→pending→claim running→record done/failed→dead) incl. runner-owned field confinement (submitted_by + branch pinned at submit; (jobs)/(job-status)/(cancel-job) confine to caller unless admin; cancel only pending and only yours)")
    (smoke "pp_eval '(submit-job …)' → (job-status id) shows submitted_by + branch pinned, then the runner fiber drives it pending→running→done with result (jobs are inspected via the job prims, NOT /data — v1 reconcile: no /data/job surface)")))

  (subsection runner
   (doc "boot-time Eio runner fiber: SKIP LOCKED claim, synthetic request context, budget, attempts/backoff, lease renewal, lease-expiry recovery. Jobs run UNBUDGETED (max_int steps) — long-running ingest/analytics ticks are the design target (upsets.ingest runs 400+ DB ops per tick); request-path evals keep the 100k sync budget (multi-user.budgets).")
   (status implemented)
   (* note (|2026-12 ralph loop 19 (campaign 4.0 plan verify) found the runner ARGS bug: eval_job wrapped job.args with Printf.sprintf "%S" (boot.ml), so a jsonb NUMBER arg was evaled as a STRING literal -(submit-job "f" 1) stores args=1 but the runner evaled (f "1") -> Interp_error -> retries -> dead. Numeric/bool/object args all failed; only JSON-string args round-tripped. 4.1 FIXED it (Jobs.eval_src): job.args is the jsonb column rendered ::text = one JSON literal, spliced DIRECTLY as the call (never %S-quoted), so numeric/object/bool args eval as their real type, and the runner surfaces the REAL error text (pp_interp_error) instead of opaque Interp_error(_). ARG-ENCODING CONTRACT on submit: the submit-job VStr branch stores the raw string as jsonb (unquoted), so a string arg must already be valid JSON -(submit-job "f" "\"hello\"") for the string hello; align submit+runner on one encoding (prim surface + Jobs.eval_src + boot runner are consistent; see image/test/jobs_test.ml args-bug suite).|) *)
   (* agent note (|2026-09-05 plan pass (operator directed; upsets.ingest consumer): eval_job previously ran jobs on Session.eval_string_rim's fresh default budget = 100k steps (lisp/lib/eval.ml default_limit) - a 2-page market poll (400+ DB ops) died budget-exceeded mid-tick. The max_int budget people remember is the BENCH prim (session.ml bench run_once), not jobs. Fix: eval_job binds Eval.current_budget to Some (make_budget ~limit:max_int ()) around the job eval (Fun.protect restores) - jobs are host-owned, submitted by authed users on their own branch, with attempts/backoff + step checkpoints as the runaway guard; request-path budgets unchanged.|) *)
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (test "image/test: jobs_test.ml runner — claim skips locked rows (second-connection FOR UPDATE); exponential backoff requeues into the future / exhausted→dead; expired lease requeued IMMEDIATELY (run_at pulled to now, attempts kept) or dead at max_attempts; a LIVE lease is never stolen by another runner; heartbeat renews the deadline and refuses a late write from a lapsed owner; same-host boot preempt recovers only THIS host's dead-pid leases")
        (test "job budget: a job stepping past 100k (long counter loop) completes done, does not die budget-exceeded - the same loop on the request path still budget-exceeds")
    (smoke "submit a job that sleeps, watch status pending→running→done with result via (job-status)")
    (smoke "submit a job whose fn raises → attempts increments, retry, eventual dead")
    (smoke "restart image mid-job → expired-lease row recovered per attempts rule; a live row on another host is left alone")))

  (subsection step-checkpoints
   (doc "(with-step \"name\" thunk) memoized by (job-id, name); with-transaction composition gives exactly-once for DB-writing steps; plain call outside jobs")
   (status implemented)
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (test "image/test: jobs_test.ml step-checkpoints — job-id fiber binding (with_current_job_id scopes current_job_id_opt; null outside a job) + checkpoint memoization round-trip on job_steps (write reads back, upsert overwrites, per (job-id,name) key independent across step names and across jobs)")
    (smoke "two-step job killed between steps via forced retry: first step not re-executed (side-effect counter row stays 1)")))

  (subsection schedules
   (doc "cron/every-ms schedules as rows; due-scan fires by submitting a job + advancing next_run; downtime collapses to one catch-up run. Per-schedule tz (migration 032, roerick 09-11 'match cron with mountain time'): tz='utc' (default, historical meaning) or tz='local' — cron fields matched against the image process zone (prod: America/Denver via the 5cec9fe TZ knob), so \"0 15 * * *\" means 15:00 Denver, DST included. DST follows vixie-cron semantics: nonexistent spring-forward times never match, repeated fall-back hours fire twice. Retires the one-shot dst-flip class (b17824c9); re-create tz-carrying schedules (work-digest) with tz=local. Matcher is an injectable calendar fn (next_cron_ms_with) so tests pin a fixed-offset UTC-6 calendar without touching process TZ")
   (status implemented)
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (test "image/test: jobs_test.ml schedules — cron next-fire computation (every-minute boundary, across-month Jan-1, impossible Feb-30 never matches) + every-ms drift/interval + spec classification (integer is not cron) + schedule CRUD/claim/advance/disable lifecycle on the schedules table + cron tz: local calendar vs utc (09:00 local = 15:00Z in MDT; matcher routes by tz string)")
    (smoke "pp_eval '(schedule \"t\" \"* * * * *\" fn args)' → runner fires a job within a minute; (schedules) shows advanced next_run + tz field")
    (smoke "(schedule \"t9\" \"0 15 * * *\" fn args \"local\") → (schedules) shows tz=\"local\" and next_run at 21:00Z (15:00 MDT)")
    (smoke "disable via (unschedule \"t\") → no further submissions")))

  (subsection non-goals
   (doc "no guest async/fibers/await; no fleet control plane; no multi-queue v1; out-of-process workers via shared claim protocol only")
   (status planned)
   (verify
    (test "conformance: no Nopales binding exposes await/promise/fiber (surface lint over prim table)")))
 )
)
